<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jinbin Huang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">jbhuang@comp.hkbu.edu.hk</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Rui Mao
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mao@szu.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jianbin Qin
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qinjianbin@szu.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">College of Computer Science and Software Engineering, Shenzhen UniversitySchool of Artificial Intelligence, Shenzhen University
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Data preparation is a foundational yet notoriously challenging component of the machine learning lifecycle, characterized by a vast combinatorial search space of potential operator sequences. While reinforcement learning (RL) offers a promising direction, existing approaches are inefficient as they fail to capture the structured, hierarchical nature of the problem. We argue that Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HRL), a paradigm that has been successful in other domains, provides a conceptually ideal yet previously unexplored framework for this task. However, a naive HRL implementation with a ‘hard hierarchy’ is prone to suboptimal, irreversible decisions. To address this, we introduce CogniQ-H, the first framework to implement a soft hierarchical paradigm for robust, end-to-end automated data preparation. CogniQ-H formulates action selection as a Bayesian inference problem. A high-level strategic prior, generated by a Large Language Model (LLM), guides exploration probabilistically. This prior is synergistically combined with a fine-grained operator quality score from a supervised Learning-to-Rank (LTR) model and a long-term value estimate from the agent’s own Q-function. This hybrid architecture allows CogniQ-H to balance strategic guidance with adaptive, evidence-based decision-making.
Through extensive experiments on 18 diverse datasets spanning multiple domains, we demonstrate that CogniQ-H achieves up to 13.9% improvement in pipeline quality and 2.8× faster convergence compared to state-of-the-art RL-based methods. The codes and datasets are available at (xxx).</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id6" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">August 24, 2026</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Data preparation is a foundational, yet notoriously labor-intensive, component of the machine learning (ML) lifecycle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. The process of transforming raw, often messy data into a clean, well-structured format suitable for model training is not a single action but a multi-stage workflow encompassing tasks like data cleaning, integration, transformation, feature engineering, and feature selection. The quality of the final ML model is profoundly dependent on the quality of this preparatory pipeline. The field of Automated Machine Learning (AutoML) seeks to automate this entire process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, with Automated Data Preparation (AutoDP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> representing one of its most significant and challenging frontiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The primary difficulty in AutoDP lies in discovering an optimal sequence of operations from a vast library of potential operators. This task is subject to a severe combinatorial explosion. To illustrate, with merely 26 available operators and pipelines of a modest length of 8, the search space contains over <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.1\times 10^{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.1\times 10^{11}</annotation></semantics></math> possible configurations (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>a). This immense scale renders brute-force search computationally infeasible and has driven the development of various optimization strategies like Bayesian optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, evolutionary algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.13710v1/intro-a.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:150/100;" width="150" height="100"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2507.13710v1/intro-b.png" id="S1.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:184/100;" width="184" height="100" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S1.F1.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2507.13710v1/intro-c.png" id="S1.F1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:112/98;" width="112" height="98" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Conceptual comparison of different search paradigms for AutoDP.
(a) All operators are available at every step, resulting in <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(26^{8})\approx 2.1\times 10^{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>26</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>2.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(26^{8})\approx 2.1\times 10^{11}</annotation></semantics></math> possible pipelines.
(b) The search is decomposed. A high-level policy and a low-level policy, reducing the space to <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(6^{5}\times 4^{3})\approx 5.0\times 10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>6</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(6^{5}\times 4^{3})\approx 5.0\times 10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>. (c) A probabilistic prior from an LLM guides the search, the final action is a synergistic fusion of the LLM prior, a fine-grained LTR score, and the RL Q-value. The search space remains as (b). However, the effective search space is drastically reduced as the synergistic policy intelligently assigns near-zero probabilities to nonsensical actions, guiding the agent through a small, high-quality region of the vast theoretical space.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, many of these approaches, especially early applications of reinforcement learning, treat the search space as “flat.” , where every operator is an atomic action at every step. This leads to critical limitations: (1) Inefficient and Unguided Exploration: Agents waste resources exploring semantically nonsensical sequences (e.g., scaling data before encoding). (2) Poor Sample Efficiency: The agent is forced to learn fundamental data science principles from scratch through costly trial-and-error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address this structural challenge, we consider the Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Framework is a natural fit for this problem, as its core principles directly align with the desired structured decision-making process. However, to the best of our knowledge, the application of HRL to construct entire data preparation pipelines remains an unexplored frontier.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Pioneering the use of HRL for this task introduces a critical design dilemma. A straightforward implementation would employ a hard hierarchy: a high-level policy selects one option (e.g., ‘Encoder’), and a low-level policy is then strictly confined to operators (Figure 1b). While this reduces complexity, suboptimal high-level choice can irrevocably prune the optimal path. For instance, if the high-level policy commits to “Encoder” when “Imputer” was necessary, the agent cannot recover. This brittleness makes a naive HRL application potentially unreliable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To harness the power of HRL while mitigating its inherent risks, we introduce <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CogniQ-H</span>, a novel framework that implements a <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">soft hierarchical paradigm</span> (Figure 1c). To our knowledge, it is the first HRL-based framework specifically designed for robust and efficient end-to-end automated data preparation. CogniQ-H leverages a Large Language Model (LLM) to provide high-level, strategic guidance in the form of a <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">probabilistic distribution</span>. This distribution acts as a flexible prior in a Bayesian framework, influencing but not constraining the final action selection. The final action selection in CogniQ-H is a synergistic fusion of three complementary signals: (1) <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM Strategic Prior:</span> A high-level probability distribution derived from an LLM. (2) <span id="S1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LTR-based Operator Score:</span> A supervised Learning-to-Rank (LTR) model provides a precise, fine-grained quality score for each individual operator. (3) <span id="S1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RL-based Value Estimation:</span> The agent’s own Q-value function, learned from past experience, represents the long-term expected reward.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This hybrid architecture allows CogniQ-H to balance pre-existing knowledge, supervised signals, and adaptive learning, leading to more robust and efficient pipeline discovery. Our contributions are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are the first to formalize and tackle the end-to-end data preparation pipelines problem within a Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning framework.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose CogniQ-H, a novel soft hierarchical paradigm that avoids the pitfalls of rigid HRL by using a probabilistic, LLM-driven prior for strategic guidance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a hybrid architecture that synergistically combines LLM-based planning, LTR-based refinement, and HRL-based learning.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Automated Data Preparation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The challenge of automating data preparation has garnered significant attention in the machine learning community <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Early AutoML systems focused primarily on model selection and hyperparameter optimization, with tools like Auto-WEKA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and Auto-sklearn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> pioneering the automated selection of learning algorithms and their configurations. TPOT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> extended this paradigm by using genetic programming to automatically design and optimize complete machine learning pipelines, including preprocessing steps.
More recent work has recognized data preparation as a critical bottleneck. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> developed an interactive system for democratizing data science through ML pipeline curation, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> introduced SAGA, a scalable framework specifically targeting the optimization of data cleaning pipelines. These systems demonstrate the growing recognition that data preparation quality directly impacts downstream model performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reinforcement Learning for AutoML</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement Learning (RL) has emerged as a promising approach for AutoML tasks due to its natural formulation as a sequential decision process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Early successes, particularly in Neural Architecture Search (NAS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, demonstrated RL’s ability to navigate vast, combinatorial search spaces. In the context of AutoDP, RL has been applied to specific sub-problems like feature selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. However, the majority of these applications adopt a "flat" RL formulation. In this setup, the agent confronts the entire library of operators at each step, with no inherent structure guiding its choices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning (HRL) provides a formal framework for learning and planning at multiple levels of temporal abstraction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Seminal works like the Options Framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> and Feudal RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> established the core principles of decomposing a task into a hierarchy of sub-tasks, enabling more efficient exploration and the learning of reusable skills. Modern HRL methods have extended these ideas with deep learning, achieving success in complex domains like robotics and navigation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Despite its success in other fields, and tis profound conceptual alignment with the nature of AutoDP, to the best of our knowledge, HRL has not been applied to the problem of constructing data preparation pipelines in an end-to-end fashion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLMs for Code and Planning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown remarkable capabilities in generating code and plans for data science tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. These approaches typically operate in an “open-loop” fashion: the LLM receives a prompt and generates a static pipeline or script. While impressive, this paradigm lacks the ability to adapt based on feedback from executing the operations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Data Preparation Pipeline)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A data preparation pipeline <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of operators <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=(o_{1},o_{2},\ldots,o_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=(o_{1},o_{2},\ldots,o_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> where each operator <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}\in\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}\in\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> transforms a dataset from one state to another. The operator library <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> includes functions for imputation, encoding, scaling, feature engineering, and feature selection.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hierarchical Action Space: Macro-Stages and Micro-Operators)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">To structure the search, we decompose the action space hierarchically.</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Macro-Stages</span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">: The global operator library </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is partitioned into a set of </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> disjoint, high-level, and semantically coherent macro-stages </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\{m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\{m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Each macro-stage represents a conceptual phase of data preparation (e.g., Imputation, Encoding, Scaling).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Micro-Operators</span><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">: Each macro-stage </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{k}\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{k}\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> governs a specific subset of operators, which we term micro-operators, </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{m_{k}}\subset\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{m_{k}}\subset\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. These micro-operators are the primitive actions the agent can execute (e.g., ‘MeanImputer’, ‘OneHotEncoder’). The global library is the union of all micro-operator sets, i.e., </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}=\bigcup_{k=1}^{K}\mathcal{A}_{m_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}=\bigcup_{k=1}^{K}\mathcal{A}_{m_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Markov Decision Process (MDP))<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We model pipeline construction as an MDP, defined by the tuple <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{T},\mathcal{R},\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{T},\mathcal{R},\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the state space (dataset characteristics), <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is the action space (operators in <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the transition function, <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward function, and <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the discount factor. The goal is to learn a policy <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes the expected cumulative reward <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}[\sum_{t=0}^{T}\gamma^{t}r_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}[\sum_{t=0}^{T}\gamma^{t}r_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hierarchical MDP (H-MDP))<span id="Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A standard two-level H-MDP decomposes the decision process. It consists of: (1) A high-level policy <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{h}(m|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{h}(m|s)</annotation></semantics></math> that selects a macro-stage (or option) <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. (2) A set of low-level policies <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\pi_{l}(\cdot|s;m)\}_{m\in\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pi_{l}(\cdot|s;m)\}_{m\in\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> selects primitive actions <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{A}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{A}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Once <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> chooses a macro <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent is committed to using <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{l}(\cdot|s;m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{l}(\cdot|s;m)</annotation></semantics></math> for a certain duration, unable to select operators from other macro-stages.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Problem Statement</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a raw dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>raw</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a downstream machine learning task <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, the goal of AutoDP is to find an optimal pipeline <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes the performance of a model <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> trained on the transformed data:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\text{Performance}(M_{\mathcal{T}}(D_{\pi}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Performance</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}=\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}\text{Performance}(M_{\mathcal{T}}(D_{\pi}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dataset after applying pipeline <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of all valid pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The primary challenge is the combinatorial nature of the search space <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Formulated as an MDP, this requires learning a policy <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math> that can navigate this vast space efficiently. As highlighted in Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition4" title="Definition 4 (Hierarchical MDP (H-MDP)). ‣ 3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, standard H-MDPs attempt to simplify this but introduce a risky, brittle hierarchy. Our work aims to overcome this by proposing a soft hierarchical approach that leverages high-level guidance without the restrictive hard commitments of traditional HRL.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>The CogniQ-H Paradigm</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CogniQ-H overcomes the limitations of traditional HRL by implementing a <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">soft hierarchical paradigm</span>. We ground this paradigm in the principles of Bayesian inference, moving beyond heuristic combinations to a principled fusion of knowledge, supervision, and experience. Instead of a rigid, two-step procedure (select macro, then select micro), our approach frames the action selection problem as inferring a posterior distribution over optimal actions, This process is visualized in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 The CogniQ-H Paradigm ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.13710v1/cogniq-h.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:286/284;" width="286" height="284" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The CogniQ-H Framework. The framework operates in three conceptual layers. (1) Macro-Stage Layer: Given the current dataset state <math id="S4.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the LLM acts as a high-level planner, generating a probabilistic distribution over macro-stages. (2) Micro-Stage Layer: This layer provides evidence for an action’s optimality. The pre-trained LTR model provides an immediate quality score <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{LTR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{LTR}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the RL Q-model, through its experience-driven training loop, learns the long-term expected reward <math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s_{t},a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s_{t},a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. (3) Synergistic Policy Layer: The final policy <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> integrates the LLM prior, LTR score, and RL Q-value. It is a normalized posterior distribution from which the final action is sampled, balancing strategic guidance with evidence-based decision-making.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>A Bayesian Inference Perspective on Action Selection</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Following the paradigm of Control as Inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, we introduce a binary latent variable <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> representing the optimality of an action. The goal is to learn a policy <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> that models the posterior probability of an action being optimal, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(a_{t}|s_{t},O=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(a_{t}|s_{t},O=1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Bayes’ rule, this posterior can be decomposed:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(a_{t}|s_{t},O=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(a_{t}|s_{t},O=1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})p(a_{t}|s_{t})}{p(O=1|s_{t})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})p(a_{t}|s_{t})}{p(O=1|s_{t})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\underbrace{p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})}_{\text{Likelihood}}\cdot\underbrace{p(a_{t}|s_{t})}_{\text{Prior}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Likelihood</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Prior</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\underbrace{p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})}_{\text{Likelihood}}\cdot\underbrace{p(a_{t}|s_{t})}_{\text{Prior}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This elegant formulation provides the theoretical scaffold for CogniQ-H. The framework’s components are designed to instantiate the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">prior</span> and the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">likelihood</span> terms.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>The LLM as a Strategic Prior</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The prior distribution, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(a_{t}|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(a_{t}|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, should encapsulate domain knowledge about which actions are sensible <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">before</span> considering any task-specific rewards. We leverage a Large Language Model (LLM) to serve as this knowledge-rich prior. The LLM acts as a high-level planner, assessing the current state <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (represented by meta-features) to produce a probability distribution over high-level macro-stages <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{LLM}}(m|s_{t})=\text{LLM-Planner}(s_{t})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>LLM-Planner</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{LLM}}(m|s_{t})=\text{LLM-Planner}(s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">From this, we define the prior probability for any specific micro-action <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by marginalizing over the macro-stages it belongs to:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(a_{t}|s_{t})\triangleq P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{t}|s_{t})=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}P_{\text{LLM}}(m|s_{t})\cdot\mathbb{I}[a_{t}\in\mathcal{A}_{m}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≜</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(a_{t}|s_{t})\triangleq P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{t}|s_{t})=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}P_{\text{LLM}}(m|s_{t})\cdot\mathbb{I}[a_{t}\in\mathcal{A}_{m}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function. This probabilistic guidance from the LLM is the cornerstone of our “soft hierarchy”.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2 </span>The Likelihood from RL and LTR</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The likelihood term, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, quantifies the probability that action <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimal. It should incorporate all available evidence of an action’s quality. CogniQ-H models this likelihood by combining two complementary signals:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experience-based Long-Term Value (RL):</span> The agent’s own action-value function, <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s_{t},a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s_{t},a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, learned through reinforcement learning, estimates the long-term cumulative reward.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervised Immediate Quality (LTR):</span> To bootstrap the learning
process and provide fine-grained guidance, we introduce a Learning-to-Rank (LTR) model. Trained offline on a large dataset of (state, action, performance) tuples, the LTR model provides a score, <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math>, that predicts the immediate utility of an action. This is particularly effective for differentiating between semantically similar operators (e.g., Mean vs. Median imputation) without costly trial-and-error. The LTR model’s limitation, however, is that it is myopic; it has no understanding of long-term planning or the downstream consequences of its recommendations.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We combine these to form the log-likelihood: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})\propto\alpha Q(s_{t},a_{t})+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})\propto\alpha Q(s_{t},a_{t})+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies the likelihood is an exponential function of their weighted sum:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})\propto\exp\left(\alpha Q(s_{t},a_{t})+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})\propto\exp\left(\alpha Q(s_{t},a_{t})+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> are hyperparameters that control the relative importance of long-term experience versus supervised immediate-quality signals in determining an action’s optimality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Synergistic Policy Formulation and Interpretation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By substituting our defined prior <a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.1.1 The LLM as a Strategic Prior ‣ 4.1 A Bayesian Inference Perspective on Action Selection ‣ 4 The CogniQ-H Paradigm ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and likelihood <a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.1.2 The Likelihood from RL and LTR ‣ 4.1 A Bayesian Inference Perspective on Action Selection ‣ 4 The CogniQ-H Paradigm ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> into the Bayesian posterior formula <a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.1 A Bayesian Inference Perspective on Action Selection ‣ 4 The CogniQ-H Paradigm ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we derive the unnormalized log-posterior (logit) for each action <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{logit}(a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>logit</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{logit}(a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\log\left[p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})\cdot p(a_{t}|s_{t})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\log\left[p(O=1|s_{t},a_{t})\cdot p(a_{t}|s_{t})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\log\left[\exp(\alpha Q(s_{t},a_{t})+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t}))\right]+\log(P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{t}|s_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\log\left[\exp(\alpha Q(s_{t},a_{t})+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t}))\right]+\log(P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{t}|s_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\alpha Q(s_{t},a_{t})+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t})+\beta\log(P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{t}|s_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\alpha Q(s_{t},a_{t})+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t})+\beta\log(P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{t}|s_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where we introduce <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> as a hyperparameter to control the strength of the prior. Applying the softmax function to these logits yields the final, normalized policy for CogniQ-H:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})=\text{softmax}(\alpha Q(s_{t},a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})=\text{softmax}(\alpha Q(s_{t},a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\beta\log P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{t}|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\beta\log P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{t}|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">This theoretically-grounded policy has a powerful interpretation: it is the optimal solution to a KL-regularized objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{*}=\arg\max_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{*}=\arg\max_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s)}\left[\alpha Q(s,a)+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(s,a)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s)}\left[\alpha Q(s,a)+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(s,a)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\beta\text{KL}\left[\pi(\cdot|s)\,||\,P_{\text{LLM}}(\cdot|s)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mtext>KL</mtext><mo>[</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\beta\text{KL}\left[\pi(\cdot|s)\,||\,P_{\text{LLM}}(\cdot|s)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">This means the agent learns to maximize a composite reward (from RL and LTR) while being regularized to stay close to the LLM’s strategic prior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide theoretical justifications for the desgin of CogniQ-H. We demonstrate how its components address the fundamental limitations in Automated Data Preparation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Provable Guidance and Pruning of Suboptimal Search Space)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The optimal policy <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for the CogniQ-H objective (Equation <a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.2 Synergistic Policy Formulation and Interpretation ‣ 4 The CogniQ-H Paradigm ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is structurally biased to assign exponentially lower probability to action sequences deemed nonsensical by the LLM prior, thereby pruning the effective search space.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The objective function to be maximized is <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s)}[R_{\text{comp}}(s,a)]-\beta\text{KL}[\pi(\cdot|s)||P_{\text{LLM}}(\cdot|s)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>comp</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s)}[R_{\text{comp}}(s,a)]-\beta\text{KL}[\pi(\cdot|s)||P_{\text{LLM}}(\cdot|s)]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{comp}}(s,a)=\alpha Q(s,a)+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>comp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{comp}}(s,a)=\alpha Q(s,a)+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math> is the composite reward.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The KL-divergence term is defined as <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{KL}[\pi||P_{\text{LLM}}]=\sum_{a\in\mathcal{O}}\pi(a|s)\log\frac{\pi(a|s)}{P_{\text{LLM}}(a|s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow></msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{KL}[\pi||P_{\text{LLM}}]=\sum_{a\in\mathcal{O}}\pi(a|s)\log\frac{\pi(a|s)}{P_{\text{LLM}}(a|s)}</annotation></semantics></math>. To maximize <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent must minimize this KL penalty.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider a semantically nonsensical action <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\text{bad}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>bad</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\text{bad}}</annotation></semantics></math> for a given state <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., applying a scaling operator to a categorical feature). A well-instructed LLM will assign a very low prior probability to this action, i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{\text{bad}}|s)\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>bad</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{\text{bad}}|s)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">If the policy were to assign a non-trivial probability to this action, i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a_{\text{bad}}|s)&gt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>bad</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a_{\text{bad}}|s)&gt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for some small <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the term <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\frac{\pi(a_{\text{bad}}|s)}{P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{\text{bad}}|s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>bad</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>bad</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\frac{\pi(a_{\text{bad}}|s)}{P_{\text{LLM}}(a_{\text{bad}}|s)}</annotation></semantics></math> would become very large and positive, leading to a significant penalty in the objective function <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, any policy <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> that optimizes this objective is heavily penalized for deviating from the prior.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The resulting optimal policy (Equation <a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.2 Synergistic Policy Formulation and Interpretation ‣ 4 The CogniQ-H Paradigm ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) shows this explicitly: <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a|s)\propto P_{\text{LLM}}(a|s)^{\beta}\exp(\frac{R_{\text{comp}}(s,a)}{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>comp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a|s)\propto P_{\text{LLM}}(a|s)^{\beta}\exp(\frac{R_{\text{comp}}(s,a)}{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>, where we can absorb scaling factors into a temperature <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. The policy probability is directly proportional to the LLM prior raised to the power of <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. A near-zero prior results in a near-zero policy probability, effectively guiding the agent away from futile exploration paths without requiring costly trial-and-error. This directly addresses the <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inefficient and Unguided Exploration</span>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Next we prove that the LTR component provides a dense reward signal that accelerates learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Accelerated Convergence through Dense Reward Shaping)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The LTR component <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{LTR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">LTR</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{LTR}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a dense potential-based reward shaping function, which is known to accelerate convergence in RL without altering the optimal policy for the long-term objective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s analyze the learning update for the Q-function. A typical update (e.g., in Q-learning) for a transition <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{t},a_{t},r_{t},s_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{t},a_{t},r_{t},s_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s_{t},a_{t})\leftarrow Q(s_{t},a_{t})+\eta(r_{t}+\max_{a^{\prime}}Q(s_{t+1},a^{\prime})-Q(s_{t},a_{t}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s_{t},a_{t})\leftarrow Q(s_{t},a_{t})+\eta(r_{t}+\max_{a^{\prime}}Q(s_{t+1},a^{\prime})-Q(s_{t},a_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">In CogniQ-H, the agent optimizes a composite reward <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{comp}}=\alpha Q(s,a)+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>comp</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{comp}}=\alpha Q(s,a)+\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>. We can view the LTR score as shaping the reward signal. The agent effectively receives an immediate, dense reward <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}_{\text{LTR}}(s_{t},a_{t})=\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}_{\text{LTR}}(s_{t},a_{t})=\gamma r_{\text{LTR}}(a_{t}|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> at every step. This reward is “dense” because the pre-trained LTR model provides a meaningful score for </span><span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span><span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text"> action in </span><span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span><span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.5" class="ltx_text"> state.
This provides a “warm start” and a continuous learning signal, drastically improving <span id="S4.SS3.p10.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">sample efficiency</span> by making every single experience more informative.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Provable Escape from Local Optima of Hard Hierarchies)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The CogniQ-H policy <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math> maintains a non-zero probability for all actions <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> at any state <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> where the Q-values and LTR scores are finite. This guarantees that the globally optimal action sequence is never irrevocably pruned from the search space.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s first formalize the policy of a hard HRL framework. The policy is <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{HRL}}(a|s)=\pi_{\text{high}}(m|s)\cdot\pi_{\text{low}}(a|s;m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>HRL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{HRL}}(a|s)=\pi_{\text{high}}(m|s)\cdot\pi_{\text{low}}(a|s;m)</annotation></semantics></math>. If the high-level policy selects macro-stage <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any action <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}\notin A_{m_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}\notin A_{m_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the probability <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{HRL}}(a_{j}|s)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>HRL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{HRL}}(a_{j}|s)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The action is permanently pruned for that step.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, consider the CogniQ-H policy from Equation <a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.2 Synergistic Policy Formulation and Interpretation ‣ 4 The CogniQ-H Paradigm ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Equation <a href="#S4.E4" title="In 4.2 Synergistic Policy Formulation and Interpretation ‣ 4 The CogniQ-H Paradigm ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>,
the policy <math id="S4.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as a standard softmax function over these logits:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})=\frac{\exp(\text{logit}(s_{t},a_{t}))}{\sum_{a^{\prime}\in\mathcal{O}}\exp(\text{logit}(s_{t},a^{\prime}_{t}))}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>logit</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>logit</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})=\frac{\exp(\text{logit}(s_{t},a_{t}))}{\sum_{a^{\prime}\in\mathcal{O}}\exp(\text{logit}(s_{t},a^{\prime}_{t}))}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">The numerator is a composition of exponential functions. The exponential function <math id="S4.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly positive for any finite <math id="S4.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Q-values <math id="S4.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math> and LTR scores <math id="S4.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{LTR}}(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>LTR</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{LTR}}(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math> are finite real numbers. The LLM prior <math id="S4.SS3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{LLM}}(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{LLM}}(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math> is designed to be non-zero. Therefore, the logit for any action <math id="S4.SS3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, and the numerator <math id="S4.SS3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(\text{logit}(a_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>logit</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(\text{logit}(a_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly positive. The denominator is a sum of strictly positive terms over the entire action space <math id="S4.SS3.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it is also strictly positive.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consequently, for any action <math id="S4.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, its probability <math id="S4.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a|s)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a|s)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. In CogniQ-H, if the agent discovers through experience that <math id="S4.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> leads to a very high long-term reward, the agent can "override" the prior based on empirical evidence from RL and LTR. This proves that the hierarchy is "soft" and that the agent always retains the flexibility to select any operator.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span> We evaluate all methods on 18 diverse, real-world datasets from the OpenML repository <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> used by DiffPrep <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. These datasets are commonly used benchmarks in AutoML research and span a variety of domains, sizes, and complexities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> We compare CogniQ-H against a strong set of baselines representing different AutoDP paradigms:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flat Standard RL</span>: A standard DQN algorithm, and a standard Q-Learning algorithm. Using an <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy exploration strategy without any external guidance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI-Pipe Of RL</span>: CtxPipe (the current state-of-the-art method) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, HAI-AI (applies the deep reinforcement learning-based AI pipeline generation algorithm proposed by HAIPipe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HRL</span>: A Hard Hierarchical RL agent (Options-DQN) where a high-level policy first selects a macro-stage, and a low-level policy is then restricted to operators within that stage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-Planner(Llama3.3 &amp; Qwen3)</span>: Zero-shot agents where large language models are prompted to generate the entire data preparation pipeline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Auto Sklearn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite></span>: Non-RL AutoML framework that uses Bayesian optimization and meta-learning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TPOT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>:</span> Non-RL AutoML framework that uses evolutionary algorithms.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Main performance comparison on 18 datasets. CogniQ-H achieves the highest average performance and the best (lowest) average rank, demonstrating its consistent superiority over a wide range of baselines. The best result on each dataset is in <span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
