<td id="S5.T1.4.21.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T1.4.21.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.06</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span> For RL-based methods, we use a Q-Learning architecture where the action space consists of 26 available operators and a DQN architecture with a 3-layer MLP for the Q-network and a replay buffer of size 5,000. The LLM used is Llama3.3, prompted with a structured description. The LTR model is a LightGBM ranker trained on an offline dataset of (state, action, performance) tuples collected from a wide range of previous experiments. The operator library consists of 26 common operators for imputation, encoding, scaling, feature preprocessing, selection, and engineering. The downstream ML model for evaluation is Logistic Regression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span> For the primary task, we report the prediction accuracy of a Logistic Regression model trained on the datasets transformed by the generated pipelines. We also report the average accuracy <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ranking</span> of each method across all datasets. To evaluate efficiency, we measure <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">running time</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pipeline length</span>. Especially, we plot the best performance found versus the number of pipelines evaluated. All results are averaged over several independent runs with different random seeds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Overall Performance Comparison</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the main experimental results, comparing CogniQ-H against all baselines across the 18 datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results clearly demonstrate the superiority of CogniQ-H. With an average score of <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.834</span> and an average rank of <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.06</span>, it significantly outperforms all other methods. Several key observations can be made:</p>
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Superiority over Standard RL</span>: CogniQ-H surpasses standard RL methods like Q-learning (0.791) and DQN (0.753). This confirms that its guided exploration mechanism is far more effective than the undirected <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy strategy, which often struggles in the vast search space, as illustrated in our motivational example (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Advantage over Hard Hierarchy</span>: CogniQ-H (Rank 2.06) also shows a clear advantage over the Hard HRL baseline (Rank 7.06). This empirically validates our central claim that the “soft hierarchy” avoids the suboptimal commitments of a rigid hierarchical structure, allowing it to find better solutions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effectiveness beyond Pure LLM</span>: While LLMs provide a strong basis for reasoning, CogniQ-H’s adaptive, experience-driven approach outperforms zero-shot LLM agents like Llama3.3 (Rank 7.72) and Qwen3 (Rank 6.83). This highlights the importance of grounding high-level knowledge with concrete, task-specific feedback from RL and LTR.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2 Overall Performance Comparison ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates the learning efficiency of CogniQ-H compared to key RL baselines. To provide a clear view of the learning dynamics, we have plotted the performance curves on three representative datasets from our evaluation suite of 18: abalone, ada_prior, and avila. The learning curve for CogniQ-H consistently shows a much steeper initial ascent, which can be attributed to two core components of our framework: the LTR model provides a dense, immediate reward signal, giving the agent a “warm start,” while the LLM prior guides exploration towards promising regions from the very beginning. In contrast, both the standard Q-Learning and the hard HRL baselines exhibit slower learning and often converge to suboptimal solutions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.13710v1/line.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Learning curves on three representative datasets (abalone, ada_prior, avila)</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Running Time and Pipeline Length.</span> Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.2 Overall Performance Comparison ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that CogniQ-H’s running time is competitive with other baseline methods. Furthermore, this competitiveness is underscored by the resulting pipelines. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 Overall Performance Comparison ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CogniQ-H: A Soft Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning Paradigm for Automated Data Preparation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, CogniQ-H discovers substantially shorter pipelines, with an average length of only <span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.72</span> operators. Shorter pipelines are not only more interpretable but also faster to execute and evaluate during the search process itself.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Comparison of average pipeline length of Cogniq-H and baselines</figcaption>
